   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>\n        <p>I&#39;m making an application to analyse time series data with Shiny. I must say that for the most part Shiny works great. The data I work with looks like:</p>\n\n<pre><code><span>                   V1     V2\n</span><span>1</span><span> </span><span>2013</span><span>-</span><span>02</span><span>-</span><span>04</span><span> </span><span>18</span><span>:</span><span>15</span><span>:</span><span>00</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span>4.746</span><span>\n</span><span>2</span><span> </span><span>2013</span><span>-</span><span>02</span><span>-</span><span>04</span><span> </span><span>18</span><span>:</span><span>20</span><span>:</span><span>00</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span>4.745</span><span>\n</span><span>3</span><span> </span><span>2013</span><span>-</span><span>02</span><span>-</span><span>04</span><span> </span><span>18</span><span>:</span><span>25</span><span>:</span><span>00</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span>4.746</span><span>\n</span><span>4</span><span> </span><span>2013</span><span>-</span><span>02</span><span>-</span><span>04</span><span> </span><span>18</span><span>:</span><span>30</span><span>:</span><span>00</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span>4.747</span><span>\n</span><span>5</span><span> </span><span>2013</span><span>-</span><span>02</span><span>-</span><span>04</span><span> </span><span>18</span><span>:</span><span>35</span><span>:</span><span>00</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span>4.747</span><span>\n</span><span>6</span><span> </span><span>2013</span><span>-</span><span>02</span><span>-</span><span>04</span><span> </span><span>18</span><span>:</span><span>40</span><span>:</span><span>00</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span>4.747</span></code></pre>\n\n<p><br></p><p>I want to plot the data in a table:</p>\n\n<pre><code><span>output$view </span><span>&lt;-</span><span> renderTable</span><span>({</span><span>\n  head</span><span>(</span><span>datasubset</span><span>(),</span><span> \n  n</span><span>=</span><span>nrow</span><span>(</span><span>datasubset</span><span>()))</span><span>\n</span><span>})</span></code></pre>\n\n<p><br></p><p>Doing so I get an error when running Shiny:</p>\n\n<pre><code><span>Error</span><span> </span><span>in</span><span> </span><span>Math</span><span>.</span><span>POSIXt</span><span>(</span><span>x </span><span>+</span><span> ifelse</span><span>(</span><span>x </span><span>==</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>))</span><span> </span><span>:</span><span> \n      </span><span>&#39;abs&#39;</span><span> </span><span>not</span><span> </span><span>defined</span><span> </span><span>for</span><span> </span><span>&quot;POSIXt&quot;</span><span> objects</span></code></pre>\n\n<p><br></p><p>Does anyone know a solution for this error?</p>\n\n    </div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "25a3F8ojJSEJ",
   "from": "Winston Chang",
   "author": "55771919c42cb6a1",
